            <ArticleTitle>Plasma disc decompression compared to physiotherapy for symptomatic contained lumbar disc herniation: A prospective randomized controlled trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>24-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pjnns.2015.11.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0028-3843(15)00191-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">To evaluate clinical outcomes with PDD as compared with patients who underwent to standard physiotherapy intervention.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One-hundred-seventy-seven randomly assigned patients with primarily radicular pain associated with a single-level lumbar contained disc herniation were enrolled. Participants received either PDD (89 patients) or conservative physiotherapy care (88 patients).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients in the PDD group had significantly greater reduction in leg pain scores and significantly improved VAS (p&lt;0.001), Oswestry Disability Index (p&lt;0.05), and 36-Item Short Form, than those in the physiotherapy group at 12 months. On subset analysis, patients achieved even better outcomes after PPD who: were younger, had a shorter period of radiculopathy, of male gender, and lower BMI. Patients with subacute pain reported better outcomes than those with chronic pain in the PDD group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Patient selection for PDD over physiotherapy favored younger patients who presented with a shorter period of pain symptoms and who had a more favorable body habitus.</AbstractText>
